Prinz defines RSI as an AI system capable of fully autonomously designing and developing its own successor. Currently, humans retain an advantage in research taste and judgment, such as choosing meaningful problems and trusting results, which AI has not yet achieved.

Most social media screenshots focus on engineering progress or minor research tasks, not on significant breakthroughs. The headline claiming that this is happening faster than expected is not supported by the actual article.

@AnthropicAI notes that their internal data shows Claude is accelerating AI development, hinting at a possible path to recursive self-improvement, and emphasizes that this rapid progress warrants greater attention.

In late November 2025, AI agentic harnesses became highly useful, leading to a surge in enterprise adoption. Based on BTOS data, the percentage of large U.S. enterprises using AI grew from about 25% to 37% between November 2025 and May 2026.

Despite this rapid adoption, unemployment rates for workers aged 20 and above have remained relatively stable. For example, for workers aged 20-24, the rate was 7.0% in September 2024, 8.3% in November 2025, and 7.1% in July 2026. This early data suggests that fears of AI causing significant job losses may be overestimated, at least so far.

@conorsen notes that the unemployment rate for the 20-24 age group has stayed roughly unchanged since the AI boom began, indicating limited immediate impact on employment in this demographic.
